<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Barghash bin Said</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1837–<a href="/years/1888">1888</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Barghash bin Said of Zanzibar · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-bu-said-dynasty">Al Bu Said dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Barghash bin Said was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> of Zanzibar from 1870 to 1888, a son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/said-bin-sultan">Said bin Sultan</a>. Under British pressure he signed the treaty of 1873 that closed the Zanzibar slave market. He built palaces including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-wonders">House of Wonders</a>, introduced piped water and steamships, and claimed a long stretch of the East African coast, but lost much of it to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scramble-for-africa">scramble for Africa</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/barghash-bin-said.jpg" alt="Barghash bin Said" width="320" height="463" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Maull &amp; Co. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Barghash_bin_Said_of_Zanzibar.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Sayyid Barghash bin Said al-Busaidi was born to the Omani sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/said-bin-sultan">Said bin Sultan</a> and an Ethiopian concubine. In 1859 a dispute broke out between him and his half-brother Majid, then <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> of Zanzibar. Their sister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emily-ruete">Sayyida Salme</a> bint Said, later known as Emily Ruete, acted at the age of fifteen as secretary of Barghash's party. With the help of an English gunboat the rising was soon put down, and Barghash was sent into exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mumbai">Bombay</a> for two years, held under virtual house arrest in the compound of the British political resident.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>After Majid's death Barghash became sultan. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emily-ruete">Emily Ruete</a> wrote in 1886 that as soon as he took the throne he imprisoned their younger brother Khalifah, next in the succession, in heavy fetters for three years, freeing him only when a sister about to go on pilgrimage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a> refused to pardon him otherwise. Barghash is credited with building much of the infrastructure of Stone Town, including public baths, a police force, roads, parks, hospitals and large administrative buildings. He dealt with diplomats from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, America, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and Portugal and was often able to play one country off against another.</p><p>He had a treaty with the British against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">slave trade</a> but was suspected of taking money from slave traders to let it go on. In June 1873 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-kirk">John Kirk</a>, the acting British Consul, received two instructions from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, one to threaten a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naval-blockade">blockade</a> unless the trade was stopped, the other not to enforce it. Kirk showed Barghash only the first, and within two weeks the sultan signed a further treaty prohibiting the slave trade and closed the great slave market.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Late in his reign the empire he had inherited broke apart. In 1884 the German Carl Peters had chiefs on the Tanganyika mainland sign documents placing their lands under German protection, ratified by an imperial letter in February 1885, and in April 1885 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/witu">Witu</a> came under German protection too. When Barghash sent troops against Witu, a German fleet forced him to give way. The British-German agreement of 29 October 1886 left him a 10-mile strip along the coast. He died on 26 March 1888, before the agreement of that year handed the coast of later Tanganyika to the Germans.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Barghash bin Said of Zanzibar” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Thuwaini prepared an expedition to assert his claim, and <b>Barghash</b>, another brother, attempted a coup in Zanzibar in 1859, but British intervention kept Majid on the throne.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sultanate-of-zanzibar">Sultanate of Zanzibar · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in Zanzibar Islands, in what is now <a href="/countries/tanzania">Tanzania</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=-5.9,39.3&amp;year=1870" aria-label="Zanzibar Islands on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5639 2384 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Barghash bin Said"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6089 2654h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6089 2654h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=-5.9,39.3&amp;year=1870">What was here in 1870? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
